CatNon School Helper
A lightweight, Progressive Web App for students. Stores all data locally on the device (no account required). Internet connection needed — NOT offline-safe.

Files:
- index.html: Main app. All UI, logic, and styles in one file
- sw.js: Service worker. Asset caching, share target handling. NOTE: the app is NOT offline-safe — core libraries (Leaflet, PeerJS, jsPDF, three.js) need network access and may fail without connectivity.
- manifest.json: PWA manifest. Allows "Add to Home Screen" and system share sheet integration
- icon.png: App icon (512×512)

Features:
Home
- Displays "CatNon Helper" blue title and app tagline
- Calculates current storage usage via navigator.storage.estimate() and IndexedDB
- Displays breakdown for stored photos and notes size/count

Gallery
- Organize photos into named albums
- Upload multiple photos at once (tap or drag & drop)
- Assign photos to albums on upload, or move them later
- Download saved photos
- Albums have a custom sort order (lower number = shown first)
- Uncategorized album collects any photos not assigned to a folder
- Tap any photo to open a full-screen lightbox
- Delete or move photos from the lightbox options menu

Notes (accessed via More screen)
- Create and edit rich-text notes
- Formatting toolbar: Undo/Redo, Bold, Italic, Underline, Strikethrough, font size (S/M/L/H), and text colors
- Notes auto-save while typing (800ms debounce)
- Rename or delete notes from the gear icon on each card
- Notes are sorted by last modified date

Network Info
- Displays current connection status, signal effective type (4G, 5G, 3G, 2G, Wi-Fi)
- Displays estimated downlink speed (Mbps), RTT latency (ms), and Data Saver state
- Interactive speed check tool to test live download throughput and ping response time

Backtracker
- Records your GPS path as a polyline on a live map (OpenStreetMap)
- Start / Stop tracking with one button
- Snap-to-location button recenters the map on your current position
- Reset button clears the recorded path
- Compass overlay shows device heading

FileDrop
- Send files directly between two devices peer-to-peer (no server, no account)
- Uses WebRTC via PeerJS — sender generates a 4-character code, receiver enters it to connect
- Progress bar on both sender and receiver
- Works across WiFi and mobile data; both devices on the same WiFi may route locally
- Recommended max file size: 100 MB

Image to PDF
- Convert images into PDF format (online via jsPDF from CDN)
- Reorder pages via direct page number inputs or Up/Down move buttons
- Reorder Gallery Modal view to inspect rotated pages and adjust sequence before exporting

QR Code Generator
- Generates QR codes for links or text with single-tap PNG download

Data Storage
All data is stored in the browser's **IndexedDB** under the database `CatNonHelperDB`. Nothing is sent to any server.

Installation (Add to Home Screen)
- Android (Chrome):
1. Open the app in Chrome
2. Tap the three-dot menu -> "Add to Home screen"
3. Confirm: The app will appear as a standalone icon

- IOS (Safari):
1. Open the app in Safari
2. Tap the Share button -> "Add to Home Screen"
3. Confirm: The app opens without browser UI

Browser Compatibility
Chrome (Android) is recommended. Safari (iOS 17+) is supported but has some limitations:
- Device orientation (compass) requires explicit user permission
- Share Target / Receiver may behave differently on older iOS versions

Known Limitations
- Photos are stored as base64 in IndexedDB — large photo libraries may hit browser storage limits
- Map tiles do not cache offline; they require an active internet connection
- FileDrop and Receiver require an internet connection for the initial peer handshake
- CDN libraries (Leaflet, PeerJS, jsPDF, three.js) fail without connectivity — the app is NOT offline-safe
- No export or cloud backup functionality
- Single-user, single-device data (no sync between browsers or devices)
